{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775378316-yF94yjV25FbCLUodeqb2","role":"assistant","created":1775378323,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The World Bank data might work. Let me try a different approach - maybe I can find the exact historical rates from a simpler source. Let me search directly in the format needed.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The question asks for \"annual average USD/INR exchange rate for the corresponding years.\" \n\nFrom historical data that I know:\n- In 1964, the USD to INR rate was around 4.76 (the Indian rupee was pegged to the USD at 4.76)\n- In 1965, after devaluation, it became about 7.5\n- In 1966, after the second devaluation, it became about 7.5\n\nWait, let me check the exact historical timeline:\n- Before June 1966: 1 USD = 4.76 INR (rupee was pegged to pound, and pound to dollar)\n- June 1966: Devaluation to 1 USD = 7.5 INR\n- 1971: Another devaluation\n\nSo the annual averages would be:\n- 1964: approximately 4.76\n- 1965: approximately 4.76 (but it changed in 1966)\n- 1966: approximately 7.5 (after June devaluation)\n\nWait, I need annual average, not end-of-year. Let me think again:\n- 1964: 4.76\n- 1965: 4.76 (still pegged)\n- 1966: After June 1966 devaluation to 7.5, so roughly 6-7.5 for the year\n\nActually wait - let me check if India had a different exchange rate in 1965 vs 1964. Looking at historical data more carefully:\n- 1948-1966: The rupee was pegged at 4.76 INR per USD until June 1966\n- June 1966: Devalued to 7.5 INR per USD\n\n\n- So 1964: 4.76\n- 1965: 4.76\n- 1966: about 6.0 (average before and after devaluation in June)\n\nI'll refine my understanding of the exchange rate timeline. The key points are the pre-1966 pegged rate and the 1966 devaluation, which impacts the annual average calculation.\n","signature":""}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
